// src/wire_sink.pdx -- libpdx-pds-parser.R223.M5 (paideia-os#2670)
//
// WireSink: parser-tier Cap'n Proto-ish wire framer that serializes
// typed shell records to a byte stream for process / host boundary
// transit, and deserializes the inverse. R223.M5 of the pipeline-lang
// umbrella (paideia-os#2487).
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE
// =============================================================================
//
// R223.M1 (pipeline.pdx) lands the pipeline stage parser. R223.M2
// (record_projection.pdx) lands `select` / `project`. R223.M3
// (filter_op.pdx) lands `where` / `filter` / `by`. R223.M4
// (sort_group_reduce.pdx) lands the three standard aggregation
// combinators (`sort by`, `group by`, `reduce`). All four landings
// operate on in-process records within one shell session.
//
// R223.M5 (THIS module) lands the SERIALIZATION layer that lets a
// pipeline stage cross a process boundary (jail/supervisor fan-out)
// or a host boundary (`find . @remote | sort by size` — the acceptance
// pipeline from paideia-os#2670). The transport underneath this
// serializer (TCP + ChaCha20-Poly1305, via `svc/remote_record_wire.pdx`
// R137.M4) is the OSARCH half; this module is the USER-SPACE SATELLITE
// half: given a (record_ptr, record_len, schema_tag) triple it emits
// a self-describing wire frame; given a wire frame it extracts the
// (record_bytes, record_len, schema_tag) triple.
//
// Per `design/terminal/semantic-shell.md` and the R223 umbrella brief,
// the shell's cross-boundary record flow needs:
//   * a flat, schema-tagged envelope that a reader can route by tag;
//   * bounded wire-image size (defensive upper bound: 1 KiB records);
//   * byte-for-byte reproducible serialization (so a fingerprint
//     computed by sender equals one computed by receiver);
//   * minimal CPU on the hot path (one zero-fill + one byte copy per
//     frame).
//
// Cap'n Proto is the industry baseline for exactly this shape. The
// R223.M5 brief directs a MINIMAL SUBSET: segment header + list of
// struct pointers + inline data. At raw M5 we land ONE segment, ONE
// struct pointer, and the inline-data payload — enough for the
// cross-process pipeline `find . @remote | sort by size` to round-trip
// its typed records fingerprint-identically between hosts. Per-field
// struct pointers (nested records, pointer sections) layer in at
// M5.1+ once R222.M1 SchemasSig can report per-field types at
// serialize time.
//
// Scope at M5:
//   * 32-byte header (magic + version + flags + segment header +
//     record_len + reserved + struct pointer tag) + variable payload
//     (record bytes zero-padded to 8-byte boundary).
//   * `_ws_init()` — pure-leaf bare `ret` boot hook (no .bss state at
//     M5; the entry exists for parallelism with the parser-family
//     init-hook convention).
//   * `_ws_serialize_record(record_ptr, record_len, schema_tag, out,
//     out_cap) -> packed_u64` — produce a frame at `out[0..required]`.
//   * `_ws_deserialize_record(wire, wire_len, out, out_cap) ->
//     packed_u64` — validate a frame and extract the plaintext
//     record_len bytes into out.
//   * `_ws_schema_hash(name_ptr, name_len) -> u64` — FNV-1a 64 of a
//     schema name byte-identical to R222.M2 `_cmd_sig_fnv1a64`, R222.M3
//     `_fi_fnv1a64`, R222.M5 `_cmdreg_fnv1a64`, R229.M5 inlined-FNV in
//     `_h_append` (same offset basis 0xCBF29CE484222325, prime
//     0x100000001B3). Returned u64 is intended for the caller to
//     either use directly or truncate to u32 (the frame's
//     `schema_tag`).
//
// Out of scope at M5 (deferred):
//   * TCP + ChaCha20-Poly1305 transport (R137.M4 OSARCH half; the
//     transport consumes/produces frames this module emits/accepts).
//   * Per-field struct pointers (nested records, pointer sections) —
//     M5.1 lands these once SchemasSig can report per-field types.
//   * Multi-segment framing — M5.2 lands the far-pointer encoding
//     for records whose combined size exceeds a single segment's
//     u16 data_words budget (128 words = 1 KiB at M5).
//   * Caps-field capture + capability tunnelling across the wire —
//     M5.3 lands the KIND_HANDLE serializer for records that carry
//     opaque cap handles rather than value-type fields.
//   * Zero-copy / scatter-gather on the receiver side — M5.4 lands
//     the direct-into-arena deserializer that bypasses the byte
//     copy loop for record payloads already resident at the correct
//     alignment.
//
// =============================================================================
// WIRE FRAME LAYOUT (M5, v1)
// =============================================================================
//
//   offset  size  field                   notes
//   ------  ----  ----------------------- -----------------------------------
//     0x00  4     magic         u32       WS_FRAME_MAGIC ('WSNK' LE
//                                          = 0x4B4E5357). Reads as the four
//                                          ASCII bytes "WSNK" sequentially.
//     0x04  2     version       u16       WS_FRAME_VERSION = 1. A wire-
//                                          format change bumps this field
//                                          and the deserializer rejects
//                                          stale frames with
//                                          WS_ERR_BAD_VERSION.
//     0x06  2     flags         u16       Reserved; zero at M5. M5.1+ uses
//                                          bit 0 for far-pointer / multi-
//                                          segment frames, bit 1 for
//                                          capability-carrying payloads.
//     0x08  4     seg_word_count u32      Brief's segment-header "word
//                                          count" field: words after the
//                                          segment header = 1 (struct
//                                          pointer word) + data_words.
//     0x0C  4     segment_id    u32       Brief's segment-header "segment
//                                          id" field: = schema_tag. Lets
//                                          a receiver route the frame
//                                          without touching the struct
//                                          pointer or the payload.
//     0x10  4     record_len    u32       Exact plaintext length (needed
//                                          for a byte-for-byte round-trip
//                                          since the Cap'n Proto struct
//                                          pointer carries only word-
//                                          granularity data_words).
//     0x14  4     reserved      u32       Zeroed; reserved for M5.1+
//                                          (schema_version, payload
//                                          flags, etc.).
//     0x18  8     struct_pointer u64      Cap'n Proto struct pointer tag:
//                                            bits  0..1   = 0 (STRUCT kind)
//                                            bits  2..31  = 0 (payload
//                                              immediately follows this
//                                              pointer word; offset-in-
//                                              words from end-of-pointer
//                                              is 0)
//                                            bits 32..47  = data_words
//                                              = ceil(record_len / 8)
//                                            bits 48..63  = 0 (no pointer
//                                              section at M5)
//     0x20  N     payload                 record_ptr bytes zero-padded
//                                          to the next 8-byte boundary.
//
//   total frame size = 32 + ceil(record_len / 8) * 8
//   with record_len <= WS_RECORD_MAX (1024) so data_words <= 128 and
//   total frame size <= 32 + 1024 = 1056.
//
// Field-by-field rationale:
//
//   * `magic` + `version`. Standard wire-format discipline (CommandSig's
//     'CSIG', FilterOp's 'FILT', DescriptorBlob's 'DESC'). 'WSNK' LE =
//     0x4B4E5357 — the u32 value whose little-endian representation
//     reads as the identifier in a hex-dump sequential scan (per the
//     Wave 125 SortGroupReduce magic-reconciliation lesson). version
//     locks the M5 commitment surface.
//
//   * `flags`. 16 bits leaves room for 16 orthogonal frame flags; M5.1
//     will claim bit 0 for the far-pointer multi-segment extension.
//     M5 serializers always emit 0; M5 deserializers always accept
//     any 16-bit value (reserved-bits-ignored discipline so an M5.1
//     frame reaching an M5 reader survives the length / struct_ptr
//     geometry gates and errors cleanly only on the unknown wire shape).
//
//   * `seg_word_count` + `segment_id`. The brief's "8B segment header
//     (word count u32, segment id u32)" verbatim. `seg_word_count` is
//     the Cap'n Proto segment-table word count = 1 (the struct pointer
//     occupies one 8-byte word) + ceil(record_len / 8) (the data section
//     occupies data_words 8-byte words). `segment_id` carries the
//     caller-supplied schema_tag; a receiver's dispatch routes on this
//     field alone without having to walk back to the struct pointer.
//
//   * `record_len`. Cap'n Proto's `data_words` field in the struct
//     pointer tag carries only word-granularity payload length (0..128
//     words). To recover the exact byte length on deserialize, we
//     carry a u32 record_len in the frame prelude. This lets the
//     round-trip be byte-for-byte reproducible even when record_len
//     is not a multiple of 8.
//
//   * `struct_pointer`. Cap'n Proto struct pointer tag with kind=0
//     (STRUCT), offset=0 (payload immediately follows the pointer word),
//     data_words=ceil(record_len/8), ptr_words=0 (no nested pointers at
//     M5). Validated byte-for-byte on deserialize; a nonzero low 32
//     bits (kind != 0 OR offset != 0) OR nonzero bits 48..63 (ptr_words
//     != 0) yields WS_ERR_BAD_STRUCT_PTR.
//
//   * `payload`. The caller's record bytes verbatim, with the tail
//     zero-padded to the next 8-byte boundary. The pad bytes are
//     EXPLICITLY ZEROED (not undefined) so two independent serializations
//     of the same (record_ptr, record_len, schema_tag) triple produce
//     byte-identical frames — the fingerprint-round-trip invariant from
//     the brief's acceptance line.
//
// Why 32 bytes of prelude:
//
//   * Fits two cache lines on x86-64 (padding + payload span exactly
//     at the 32-byte boundary that opens the second line).
//   * Power-of-two prelude → the (frame_bytes - 32) / 8 = data_words
//     relation uses `shr rN, 3` directly.
//   * Leaves 8 bytes of reserved tail (`reserved` u32 + the second
//     half of `struct_pointer` if a future wire-format change needs
//     two-pointer frames) for a one-M-version-bump path to the next
//     wire shape.
//
// =============================================================================
// PUBLIC API
// =============================================================================
//
//   _ws_init() -> ()
//
//     Idempotent lazy boot hook. M5 holds NO .bss state (every frame is
//     caller-owned on both the serialize and deserialize paths), so
//     the body is a bare `ret`. Kept for parallelism with the parser-
//     family init-hook convention (`_ds_init` R222.M8, `_dm_init`
//     R222.M6, `_cmdreg_init` R222.M5) so a later landing that adds
//     .bss (per-connection sequence counters, schema_tag intern table)
//     will flesh out this body without an ABI break.
//
//     Register map: () -> rax (ignored).
//
//   _ws_serialize_record(record_ptr, record_len, schema_tag, out, out_cap)
//                                                       -> packed_u64
//
//     Produce a wire frame at `out[0..required]` from the plaintext
//     record at `record_ptr[0..record_len]` tagged with `schema_tag`.
//
//     Steps:
//       1. Validate record_ptr != 0 (WS_ERR_BAD_RECORD on failure).
//       2. Validate record_len <= WS_RECORD_MAX (1024)
//          (WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE on failure).
//       3. Validate out != 0, out_cap >= 32 + ceil(record_len/8)*8
//          (WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL on failure).
//       4. Zero-fill the entire frame region (header + payload + pad).
//       5. Stamp magic 'WSNK' u32 at [+0], version u16 lo byte 1 at
//          [+4] (hi byte zero from step 4), seg_word_count u32 at [+8]
//          = 1 + data_words, segment_id u32 at [+12] = schema_tag,
//          record_len u32 at [+16] = record_len input, struct_pointer
//          u64 at [+24] with data_words in bits 32..47.
//       6. Copy record_len bytes from record_ptr to [out + 32]
//          (byte-by-byte).
//       7. Return (frame_bytes << 32) | WS_OK where frame_bytes = 32
//          + ceil(record_len / 8) * 8.
//
//     Returns packed_u64 whose high 32 is the bytes_written and low
//     32 is the status code:
//       WS_OK                   (0)       on success
//       WS_ERR_BAD_RECORD       (0x5200)  record_ptr == 0
//       WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE (0x5201)  record_len > WS_RECORD_MAX
//       WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL    (0x5202)  out == 0 or out_cap too small
//
//     Register map (SysV):
//       _ws_serialize_record(rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8) -> rax
//         rdi = record_ptr
//         rsi = record_len
//         rdx = schema_tag (low 32 bits used)
//         rcx = out
//         r8  = out_cap
//         rax = (bytes_written << 32) | status
//
//   _ws_deserialize_record(wire, wire_len, out, out_cap) -> packed_u64
//
//     Validate a wire frame at `wire[0..wire_len]` and extract the
//     plaintext record bytes into `out[0..record_len]`.
//
//     Steps:
//       1. Validate wire != 0, wire_len >= 32, out != 0
//          (WS_ERR_BAD_FRAME on failure).
//       2. Validate magic 'WSNK' at [+0] (WS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC on
//          failure).
//       3. Validate version lo byte 1 at [+4] and hi byte 0 at [+5]
//          (WS_ERR_BAD_VERSION on failure).
//       4. Read seg_word_count u32 at [+8]; validate 1 <= it <= 129
//          (WS_ERR_BAD_SEG_HEADER on failure).
//       5. Read record_len u32 at [+16]; validate <= WS_RECORD_MAX
//          (WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE on failure).
//       6. Compute data_words_expected = ceil(record_len / 8); validate
//          seg_word_count == 1 + data_words_expected
//          (WS_ERR_BAD_SEG_HEADER on failure).
//       7. Read struct_pointer u64 at [+24]; validate low 32 bits == 0
//          (kind=0, offset=0); validate bits 48..63 == 0 (ptr_words=0);
//          validate bits 32..47 == data_words_expected
//          (WS_ERR_BAD_STRUCT_PTR on failure).
//       8. Validate wire_len >= 32 + data_words_expected * 8
//          (WS_ERR_BAD_FRAME on failure).
//       9. Validate out_cap >= record_len (WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL on
//          failure).
//      10. Copy record_len bytes from [wire + 32] to out
//          (byte-by-byte).
//      11. Return (record_len << 32) | WS_OK.
//
//     Returns packed_u64 whose high 32 is the bytes_written (=
//     record_len on success, 0 on error) and low 32 is the status code:
//       WS_OK                   (0)       on success
//       WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL    (0x5202)  out == 0 or out_cap < record_len
//       WS_ERR_BAD_FRAME        (0x5203)  wire == 0, wire_len < 32,
//                                         or wire_len < frame geometry
//       WS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC        (0x5204)  magic != 'WSNK'
//       WS_ERR_BAD_VERSION      (0x5205)  version != 1
//       WS_ERR_BAD_SEG_HEADER   (0x5206)  seg_word_count geometry fails
//       WS_ERR_BAD_STRUCT_PTR   (0x5207)  struct pointer kind/offset/
//                                         ptr_words/data_words bad
//       WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE (0x5201)  wire's record_len exceeds
//                                         WS_RECORD_MAX
//
//     Register map (SysV):
//       _ws_deserialize_record(rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx) -> rax
//         rdi = wire
//         rsi = wire_len
//         rdx = out
//         rcx = out_cap
//         rax = (bytes_written << 32) | status
//
//   _ws_schema_hash(name_ptr, name_len) -> u64
//
//     FNV-1a 64 of the schema name bytes. Byte-for-byte identical to
//     R222.M2 `_cmd_sig_fnv1a64`, R222.M3 `_fi_fnv1a64`, R222.M5
//     `_cmdreg_fnv1a64`, R229.M5 inlined FNV in `_h_append`. Pure leaf;
//     computes offset_basis 0xCBF29CE484222325 then for each byte
//     XORs the byte into the accumulator and multiplies by prime
//     0x100000001B3. A zero-length input returns the offset_basis
//     unchanged (standard FNV behaviour).
//
//     The caller derives a 32-bit `schema_tag` from this 64-bit hash
//     by truncating (`u32 cast` or `and r, 0xFFFFFFFF`). The 32-bit
//     width is tight for a universe-wide schema namespace (2^32 ~=
//     4.3e9 distinct tags), but comfortable for a per-session
//     catalogue (the shell's registered schemas number in the
//     hundreds, not billions). Collision probability at 1000 schemas
//     per session is ~1.2e-4 (birthday approximation 1000^2 / 2^33);
//     a session that hits a collision reports it via the registry's
//     dedup pass at session boot and the user retitles.
//
//     Register map (SysV):
//       _ws_schema_hash(rdi, rsi) -> rax
//         rdi = name_ptr
//         rsi = name_len
//         rax = FNV-1a 64 hash
//
// =============================================================================
// STATUS BAND -- 0x5200..0x52FF
// =============================================================================
//
//   WS_OK                     = 0
//   WS_ERR_BAD_RECORD         = 0x5200
//   WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE   = 0x5201
//   WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL      = 0x5202
//   WS_ERR_BAD_FRAME          = 0x5203
//   WS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC          = 0x5204
//   WS_ERR_BAD_VERSION        = 0x5205
//   WS_ERR_BAD_SEG_HEADER     = 0x5206
//   WS_ERR_BAD_STRUCT_PTR     = 0x5207
//
// The 0x5200..0x52FF range is reserved for the R223.M5 wire-sink
// band. Slots 0x5208..0x52FF reserved for R223.M5.1+ (far-pointer
// errors, nested-record errors, cap-handle serialization errors,
// schema_tag conflicts, etc.).
//
// The 0x5200..0x52FF band carves a non-adjacent kilo-step past Wave
// 126's 0x5100..0x51FF (R229.M5 History), 0x5000..0x50FF (R224.M6
// PipelineLambda), and 0x4F00..0x4FFF (R222.M8 DescribeSig). The
// four prior R223 submodule bands (0x2B00 Pipeline, 0x3400
// RecordProjection, 0x4C00 FilterOp, 0x4D00 SortGroupReduce) remain
// error-disjoint so a downstream dispatcher's compound error path
// can distinguish wire-sink faults from every other R223 submodule
// fault.
//
// =============================================================================
// paideia-as ENCODER DISCIPLINE (ABSOLUTE)
// =============================================================================
//
//   - No `test rN, rN` -- every zero-check is `cmp reg, 0; je/jne`.
//   - No `and rN, imm64` -- bit-isolation uses imm32 (sign-extend
//     safe when bit 31 clear) and imm8 variants. The one imm32 mask
//     used here is `and rax, 0xFFFF` (0x0000FFFF, bit 31 clear) for
//     the struct_pointer data_words extract on deserialize; the two
//     imm8 masks are `and rax, 1` and `and rax, 7` (not actually
//     needed in this file at M5 — the alignment path uses `add +7
//     then shr 3` + `shl 3` pairs).
//   - No 2-op `imul rN, imm` -- the frame-size compute uses `add +7;
//     shr 3; shl 3` (round-up to 8-byte boundary) and `shl 3`
//     (data_words * 8); the FNV multiply is one-op `mul rcx`
//     (precedent: _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 line 450).
//   - Variable shift via `shr rN, cl` / `shl rN, cl` is NOT USED at
//     M5 (every shift amount in this module is a compile-time
//     constant: 1, 2, 3, 8, 32, 48).
//   - No multiline `pub let = "..."` -- single-line justifications.
//   - Module basename `WireSink` matches `wire_sink.pdx`.
//   - Labels prefixed `ws_` (grep-confirmed unique against the
//     parser-tier modules' prior prefixes u8n_, gi_, wp_, sch_sig_,
//     cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, pl_, rp_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, cmdreg_,
//     hm_, rt_, dl_, se_, te_, cl_, dm_, fo_, sgr_, jc_, fh_, ast_,
//     sm_, ls_, lex_, eu_, h_, pl_lam_, ds_). The lone pre-existing
//     `ws_`-family labels (`bdw_ws_*` in `src/kernel/core/fs/pdxfs/
//     bdev_write.pdx`, `phc_ws_*` in `src/kernel/core/ipc/pcie_
//     hotplug_channel.pdx`, `tui_present_k_rows_dirty` in `src/
//     kernel/core/tui/canvas_present.pdx`) are function-scoped under
//     distinct parent prefixes (`bdw_`, `phc_`, `tui_present_`) and
//     live in disjoint kernel-side linker namespaces — the same
//     disjointness pattern cap_row.pdx and cmd_registry.pdx cite for
//     their own prefixes. The public API still owns the `_ws_*`
//     entry-point namespace, which is distinct from `ws_*` as the
//     function-local label namespace. Never bare `loop` / `if` /
//     `let` / `fn` / `ret` (per reference_paideia_as_reserved_labels).
//   - All `cmp reg, imm` immediates (0, 1, 7, 8, 32, 128, 129, 1024,
//     magic 0x4B4E5357, status codes 0x5200..0x5207, field offsets
//     0, 4, 5, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32) fit imm32 with bit 31 clear
//     (0x4B4E5357 < 0x80000000 so cmp-imm32 is sign-safe). No
//     immediate in this file needs the imm64 encoding except the
//     two FNV constants (0xCBF29CE484222325, 0x100000001B3) which
//     use the standard `mov r64, imm64` encoding (precedent:
//     _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 lines 437-438).
//   - Byte loads via `xor rN, rN; mov_b rN, [base + idx]` (zero-
//     extend into the full qword).
//   - Byte stores via `mov_b [dst + disp], reg` (writes low 8 bits).
//   - u32 loads via `xor rN, rN; mov_d rN, [base + disp]` (zero-
//     extend into the full qword).
//   - u32 stores via `mov_d [dst + disp], reg` (writes low 32 bits).
//   - u64 loads/stores via plain `mov`.
//   - No chained-displacement addressing: `[base + off + idx]` is
//     precomputed into a scratch via `add` first, then dereferenced
//     via `[rN + 0]`. Precedent: command_sig.pdx name-copy loops.
//     The single exception is `_ws_schema_hash`'s FNV loop which
//     uses the two-register addressing form `mov_b r11, [r8 + r10]`
//     byte-for-byte identical to `_cmd_sig_fnv1a64` (line 446).
//
// =============================================================================
// REGISTER-LIVENESS DISCIPLINE
// =============================================================================
//
// Every entry point in this module is a PURE LEAF -- no nested CALL,
// no callee-save push, no stack frame, no `sub rsp, N`. The r11
// clobber contract never activates because no CALL fires.
//
//   * `_ws_init`: bare `ret`. No regs touched.
//   * `_ws_serialize_record`: 5 inputs in rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8;
//     scratch in rax, r9, r10, r11. rdi (record_ptr) and rcx (out)
//     are the two base pointers held unchanged across the zero-fill
//     and payload-copy loops; rsi (record_len) is read-only throughout
//     (used in the payload-copy loop terminator and in the return
//     frame-size recompute); rdx (schema_tag) is stored through to
//     [out + 12] early then free; r8 (out_cap) is read-only after
//     the capacity validation; r9 holds data_words across the header
//     stamp (dies after struct_ptr stamp); r10 holds required_bytes
//     across the zero-fill loop (dies after zero-fill); r11 is the
//     loop index for zero-fill and payload-copy.
//   * `_ws_deserialize_record`: 4 inputs in rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx;
//     scratch in rax, r8, r9, r10, r11. rdi (wire) and rdx (out) are
//     the two base pointers held unchanged across all validation and
//     the payload-copy loop; rsi (wire_len) is read-only; rcx
//     (out_cap) is read-only after the capacity validation; r8 holds
//     seg_word_count across validation; r9 holds record_len across
//     validation and the payload-copy loop (used as copy terminator
//     and return bytes-written); r10 holds data_words_expected
//     across the geometry validation; r11 holds the struct_pointer
//     u64 across its three sub-field validations, then is reused as
//     the loop index.
//   * `_ws_schema_hash`: 2 inputs in rdi, rsi; scratch in rax, rcx
//     (held = FNV prime), rdx (clobbered each mul), r8 (ptr saved),
//     r9 (len saved), r10 (loop idx), r11 (byte scratch).
//     Byte-for-byte register map match to `_cmd_sig_fnv1a64`.
//
// =============================================================================
// LAZY-INIT DISCIPLINE
// =============================================================================
//
// Not applicable at M5: this module holds no .bss state. The wire
// frame is caller-owned on both the serialize and deserialize paths;
// the schema hash operates as a pure byte walk over a caller-supplied
// name buffer. `_ws_init` is a bare `ret` placeholder; a later landing
// that adds .bss (per-connection sequence counters, a schema_tag
// intern table, a per-session serialization-fault counter) will flesh
// it out behind the same name.
//
// =============================================================================
// LINKER DEPENDENCIES
// =============================================================================
//
// NO external cross-library linker dependency. The transport
// (svc/remote_record_wire.pdx R137.M4 OSARCH half) consumes/produces
// the frames this module emits/accepts; the two halves live in
// disjoint linker namespaces (kernel-side vs user-space satellite).
// NO intra-library cross-module linker dependency. The schema hash
// is a self-contained FNV-1a 64 leaf byte-identical to the parser-
// family's four prior FNV leaves (`_cmd_sig_fnv1a64`,
// `_fi_fnv1a64`, `_cmdreg_fnv1a64`, inlined in `_h_append`); each
// leaf is replicated locally per the parser-family disjoint-leaf
// precedent so this module can be linked standalone against a bare
// parser-tier object graph without pulling in R222.M2 CommandSig,
// R222.M3 FunctorInstance, R222.M5 CmdRegistry, or R229.M5 History.
//
// R223.M5.1+ (per-field struct pointers via SchemasSig fold) will
// introduce a one-way intra-library STRUCTURAL dependency on R222.M1
// SchemasSig (the per-field type codes) but STILL no linker-visible
// dependency because the SchemasSig body buffer is handed in as an
// opaque caller-managed pointer.
//
// =============================================================================
// Upstream: paideia-os#2487 (R223 umbrella), paideia-os#2670 (THIS
// ticket, R223.M5). Substrate: paideia-os#2666 (R223.M1 Pipeline),
// paideia-os#2667 (R223.M2 RecordProjection), paideia-os#2668
// (R223.M3 FilterOp), paideia-os#2669 (R223.M4 SortGroupReduce),
// paideia-os#2659 (R222.M2 CommandSig — FNV discipline). Transport
// companion: paideia-os#2636 (R137.M4 remote_record_wire, OSARCH
// half). Design doc: design/shell/wire-format-capnproto.md. Related:
// design/shell/remote-record-wire.md (the ChaCha20-Poly1305 framer
// one layer below), design/terminal/semantic-shell.md §cross-boundary
// pipelines (the user-facing acceptance target).

module WireSink = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1 -- STATUS CODES (low 32 bits of return)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Small-value range 0x5200..0x52FF, reserved for the R223.M5
  // wire-sink band. Disjoint from every prior landed R223 submodule
  // band:
  //   Pipeline            0x2B00..0x2BFF (R223.M1)
  //   RecordProjection    0x3400..0x34FF (R223.M2)
  //   FilterOp            0x4C00..0x4CFF (R223.M3)
  //   SortGroupReduce     0x4D00..0x4DFF (R223.M4)
  //
  // Grep confirmation at R223.M5 landing time:
  //   grep -rE '0x520[0-7]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
  //     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
  //        design/shell/wire-format-capnproto.md.

  pub let WS_OK                     : u64 = 0
  pub let WS_ERR_BAD_RECORD         : u64 = 0x5200
  pub let WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE   : u64 = 0x5201
  pub let WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL      : u64 = 0x5202
  pub let WS_ERR_BAD_FRAME          : u64 = 0x5203
  pub let WS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC          : u64 = 0x5204
  pub let WS_ERR_BAD_VERSION        : u64 = 0x5205
  pub let WS_ERR_BAD_SEG_HEADER     : u64 = 0x5206
  pub let WS_ERR_BAD_STRUCT_PTR     : u64 = 0x5207

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 2 -- LAYOUT CONSTANTS
  // ==========================================================================

  // Prelude bytes before the payload. Equal to the offset of the
  // payload (= struct_pointer offset + 8 = 24 + 8 = 32).
  pub let WS_FRAME_HEADER_BYTES     : u64 = 32

  // Wire-format version. Bumped on any wire-format change.
  pub let WS_FRAME_VERSION          : u64 = 1

  // Magic: 'WSNK' little-endian = 0x4B4E5357. Reads as the 4-byte
  // ASCII string "WSNK" in a hex dump (per the Wave 125
  // SortGroupReduce magic-reconciliation lesson).
  pub let WS_FRAME_MAGIC            : u64 = 0x4B4E5357

  // Defensive upper bound on record_len. At M5 the far-pointer
  // extension that lets a frame grow past one segment is deferred
  // (R223.M5.2 scope); records exceeding this bound return
  // WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE on serialize AND on deserialize.
  pub let WS_RECORD_MAX             : u64 = 1024

  // Max data_words = WS_RECORD_MAX / 8 = 128. seg_word_count max
  // (= 1 struct ptr word + data_words) = 129.
  pub let WS_DATA_WORDS_MAX         : u64 = 128
  pub let WS_SEG_WORD_COUNT_MAX     : u64 = 129

  // Wire frame field offsets (byte offsets from frame base).
  pub let WS_OFF_MAGIC              : u64 = 0     // u32
  pub let WS_OFF_VERSION            : u64 = 4     // u16
  pub let WS_OFF_FLAGS              : u64 = 6     // u16
  pub let WS_OFF_SEG_WORD_COUNT     : u64 = 8     // u32 (brief seg hdr lo)
  pub let WS_OFF_SEGMENT_ID         : u64 = 12    // u32 (brief seg hdr hi)
  pub let WS_OFF_RECORD_LEN         : u64 = 16    // u32
  pub let WS_OFF_RESERVED           : u64 = 20    // u32
  pub let WS_OFF_STRUCT_POINTER     : u64 = 24    // u64
  pub let WS_OFF_PAYLOAD            : u64 = 32    // N bytes

  // ==========================================================================
  // _ws_init() -> ()
  //
  // Idempotent lazy boot hook. M5 holds no .bss state so the body is
  // a bare `ret`. Kept for parallelism with the parser-family init-
  // hook convention; a later landing that adds .bss will flesh this
  // out behind the same name without an ABI break.
  //
  // Pure leaf -- no callee-save push, no stack frame, no nested CALL.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _ws_init : () -> () !{} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R223.M5 (#2670): _ws_init is a bare ret at this landing. The module holds no .bss state at M5 (every wire frame is caller-owned on both the serialize and deserialize paths; the schema hash operates as a pure byte walk over a caller-supplied name buffer). The entry point exists for parallelism with the parser-family init-hook convention (_ds_init R222.M8, _dm_init R222.M6, _cmdreg_init R222.M5) so a later landing that adds .bss (per-connection sequence counters, a schema_tag intern table, a per-session serialization-fault counter) does not break the API. Pure leaf; zero pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL. No cmp immediates; no byte loads; no stores. Labels prefixed ws_ini_ (none at M5).",
      block: {
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _ws_serialize_record(record_ptr, record_len, schema_tag, out, out_cap)
  //                                                      -> packed_u64
  //
  //   rdi = record_ptr    (plaintext record base)
  //   rsi = record_len    (plaintext length; 0..WS_RECORD_MAX)
  //   rdx = schema_tag    (caller-supplied; low 32 bits used)
  //   rcx = out           (ptr to caller buffer; >= 32 + ceil(len/8)*8)
  //   r8  = out_cap       (byte capacity of out)
  //   rax = (bytes_written << 32) | status
  //
  // Produce a 32-byte-header-plus-padded-payload wire frame.
  //
  // Flow:
  //   1. Validate record_ptr != 0, record_len <= 1024, out != 0,
  //      out_cap >= required.
  //   2. Zero-fill the required bytes.
  //   3. Stamp magic / version / seg_word_count / segment_id /
  //      record_len / struct_pointer header fields.
  //   4. Copy record_len bytes from record_ptr to [out + 32].
  //   5. Return (frame_bytes << 32) | WS_OK.
  //
  // Pure leaf -- no callee-save push, no stack frame, no nested CALL.
  // Register plan: rdi, rcx held unchanged; rsi held read-only; rdx
  // store-through at step 3; r8 dead after step 1; r9/r10/r11/rax
  // scratch. Labels prefixed ws_ser_.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _ws_serialize_record : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (record_ptr: u64) (record_len: u64) (schema_tag: u64) (out: u64) (out_cap: u64)
       -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R223.M5 (#2670): _ws_serialize_record produces a Cap'n Proto-ish wire frame at [out, out + 32 + ceil(record_len/8)*8) from a plaintext record at [record_ptr, record_ptr + record_len) tagged with schema_tag. Pure leaf; zero callee-save pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL. Flow: (1) Validate record_ptr != 0 (cmp rdi, 0; je ws_ser_bad_record). (2) Validate record_len <= WS_RECORD_MAX (1024) (cmp rsi, 1024; ja ws_ser_record_too_large). (3) Validate out != 0 (cmp rcx, 0; je ws_ser_oos). (4) Compute data_words = (record_len + 7) >> 3 into r9 (mov r9, rsi; add r9, 7; shr r9, 3). (5) Compute required_bytes = 32 + data_words * 8 into r10 (mov r10, r9; shl r10, 3; add r10, 32). (6) Validate out_cap >= required_bytes (cmp r8, r10; jb ws_ser_oos). (7) Zero-fill required_bytes via a u64 stepping loop: xor r11, r11; each iter cmp r11, r10; jae ws_ser_hdr; mov r8, rcx; add r8, r11; xor rax, rax; mov [r8 + 0], rax; add r11, 8; jmp. r8 is dead after the capacity validation so we reuse it as the loop-local address scratch. (8) Stamp magic 'WSNK' u32 at [out+0] (mov rax, 0x4B4E5357; mov_d [rcx + 0], rax); 0x4B4E5357 < 0x80000000 so mov-imm32 is sign-safe. (9) Stamp version u16 lo byte 1 at [out+4] (mov rax, 1; mov_b [rcx + 4], rax); the hi byte at [+5] stays zero from the zero-fill, matching the frame-version field u16 = 1. The flags u16 at [+6..+8] stays zero from the zero-fill. (10) Stamp seg_word_count u32 at [out+8] = 1 + data_words (mov rax, r9; add rax, 1; mov_d [rcx + 8], rax). (11) Stamp segment_id u32 at [out+12] = schema_tag (mov_d [rcx + 12], rdx); rdx's low 32 are written, matching the frame segment_id u32. (12) Stamp record_len u32 at [out+16] = rsi (mov_d [rcx + 16], rsi); rsi's low 32 are written, matching the frame record_len u32 (bounded <= 1024 by step 2). The reserved u32 at [+20..+24] stays zero from the zero-fill. (13) Stamp struct_pointer u64 at [out+24]: pack data_words (<= 128, fits u16) into bits 32..47 by (mov rax, r9; shl rax, 32; mov [rcx + 24], rax). Low 32 bits are 0 (kind=0 at bits 0..1, offset=0 at bits 2..31). Bits 48..63 stay zero via the zero-fill. (14) Payload copy loop: xor r11, r11; each iter cmp r11, rsi; jae ws_ser_done; compute src addr = record_ptr + r11 into r8 (mov r8, rdi; add r8, r11); load byte into rax (xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [r8 + 0]); compute dst addr = out + 32 + r11 into r9 (mov r9, rcx; add r9, 32; add r9, r11); store byte (mov_b [r9 + 0], rax); add r11, 1; jmp. Clobbers r9 (data_words no longer needed after struct_pointer stamp) and r8 (dead since zero-fill). (15) Return packed_u64 = (required_bytes << 32) | WS_OK: recompute required_bytes from rsi via (mov rax, rsi; add rax, 7; shr rax, 3; shl rax, 3; add rax, 32); shl rax, 32 packs bytes_written into the high 32 bits with status 0 in the low 32. Error returns: ws_ser_bad_record stamps rax := 0x5200; ws_ser_record_too_large stamps 0x5201; ws_ser_oos stamps 0x5202. All cmp immediates (0, 7, 1024, magic 0x4B4E5357, status codes 0x5200..0x5202, field offsets 0, 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32) fit imm32 with bit 31 clear. No test; no and-imm64; no 2-op imul-r-imm; no multiline let. Labels prefixed ws_ser_.",
      block: {
        // ---- Input validation --------------------------------------------
        // record_ptr != 0.
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  ws_ser_bad_record;

        // record_len <= WS_RECORD_MAX (1024).
        cmp rsi, 1024;
        ja  ws_ser_record_too_large;

        // out != 0.
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je  ws_ser_oos;

        // data_words = (record_len + 7) >> 3.
        mov r9, rsi;
        add r9, 7;
        shr r9, 3;                             // r9 = data_words

        // required_bytes = 32 + data_words * 8.
        mov r10, r9;
        shl r10, 3;                            // r10 = padded_len
        add r10, 32;                           // r10 = required_bytes

        // out_cap >= required_bytes.
        cmp r8, r10;
        jb  ws_ser_oos;

        // ---- Zero-fill required bytes ------------------------------------
        xor r11, r11;                          // i = 0
      ws_ser_zfill:
        cmp r11, r10;
        jae ws_ser_hdr;
        mov r8, rcx;
        add r8, r11;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r8 + 0], rax;                     // u64 zero store
        add r11, 8;
        jmp ws_ser_zfill;

      ws_ser_hdr:
        // ---- Header stamp ------------------------------------------------
        // magic u32 'WSNK' at [+0].
        mov rax, 0x4B4E5357;                   // WS_FRAME_MAGIC
        mov_d [rcx + 0], rax;

        // version u16 low byte 1 at [+4]; hi byte zero from zero-fill.
        mov rax, 1;                            // WS_FRAME_VERSION
        mov_b [rcx + 4], rax;
        // flags u16 at [+6..+8] stays zero.

        // seg_word_count u32 at [+8] = 1 + data_words.
        mov rax, r9;
        add rax, 1;
        mov_d [rcx + 8], rax;

        // segment_id u32 at [+12] = schema_tag (low 32 bits of rdx).
        mov_d [rcx + 12], rdx;

        // record_len u32 at [+16] = rsi (bounded <= 1024).
        mov_d [rcx + 16], rsi;
        // reserved u32 at [+20..+24] stays zero.

        // struct_pointer u64 at [+24]:
        //   bits 0..1 = 0 (STRUCT kind)
        //   bits 2..31 = 0 (payload immediately follows this ptr word)
        //   bits 32..47 = data_words (r9's low 16; r9 <= 128)
        //   bits 48..63 = 0 (no ptr section)
        mov rax, r9;
        shl rax, 32;                           // move data_words to bits 32..47
        mov [rcx + 24], rax;

        // ---- Payload copy loop ------------------------------------------
        xor r11, r11;                          // i = 0
      ws_ser_pay:
        cmp r11, rsi;
        jae ws_ser_done;
        // src = record_ptr + i
        mov r8, rdi;
        add r8, r11;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r8 + 0];
        // dst = out + 32 + i
        mov r9, rcx;
        add r9, 32;
        add r9, r11;
        mov_b [r9 + 0], rax;
        add r11, 1;
        jmp ws_ser_pay;

      ws_ser_done:
        // Return (required_bytes << 32) | WS_OK (= 0).
        // Recompute required_bytes = 32 + ((record_len + 7) & ~7).
        mov rax, rsi;
        add rax, 7;
        shr rax, 3;
        shl rax, 3;
        add rax, 32;                           // rax = required_bytes
        shl rax, 32;                           // bytes_written in high 32
        ret;

      ws_ser_bad_record:
        mov rax, 0x5200;                       // WS_ERR_BAD_RECORD
        ret;

      ws_ser_record_too_large:
        mov rax, 0x5201;                       // WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE
        ret;

      ws_ser_oos:
        mov rax, 0x5202;                       // WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _ws_deserialize_record(wire, wire_len, out, out_cap) -> packed_u64
  //
  //   rdi = wire          (wire frame base)
  //   rsi = wire_len      (bytes available at wire)
  //   rdx = out           (plaintext output buffer)
  //   rcx = out_cap       (byte capacity of out; >= record_len)
  //   rax = (bytes_written << 32) | status
  //
  // Validate a wire frame and extract the plaintext record bytes.
  //
  // Flow:
  //   1. Validate wire != 0, wire_len >= 32, out != 0.
  //   2. Validate magic 'WSNK' at [+0], version 1 at [+4..+6].
  //   3. Read seg_word_count u32 at [+8]; validate 1..129.
  //   4. Read record_len u32 at [+16]; validate <= 1024.
  //   5. Compute data_words_expected = (record_len + 7) >> 3;
  //      validate seg_word_count == 1 + data_words_expected.
  //   6. Read struct_pointer u64 at [+24]; validate low 32 == 0,
  //      bits 48..63 == 0, bits 32..47 == data_words_expected.
  //   7. Validate wire_len >= 32 + data_words_expected * 8.
  //   8. Validate out_cap >= record_len.
  //   9. Copy record_len bytes from [wire + 32] to out.
  //  10. Return (record_len << 32) | WS_OK.
  //
  // Pure leaf -- no callee-save push, no stack frame, no nested CALL.
  // Labels prefixed ws_des_.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _ws_deserialize_record : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (wire: u64) (wire_len: u64) (out: u64) (out_cap: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R223.M5 (#2670): _ws_deserialize_record validates a wire frame at [wire, wire + wire_len) and extracts the plaintext record bytes into [out, out + record_len). Pure leaf; zero callee-save pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL. Flow: (1) Validate wire != 0 (cmp rdi, 0; je ws_des_bad_frame); wire_len >= 32 (cmp rsi, 32; jb ws_des_bad_frame); out != 0 (cmp rdx, 0; je ws_des_bad_frame). (2) Read magic u32 at [+0]: xor rax, rax; mov_d rax, [rdi + 0]; cmp rax, 0x4B4E5357; jne ws_des_bad_magic. 0x4B4E5357 < 0x80000000 so cmp-imm32 is sign-safe. (3) Read version lo byte at [+4]: xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rdi + 4]; cmp rax, 1; jne ws_des_bad_version. Read version hi byte at [+5]: xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rdi + 5]; cmp rax, 0; jne ws_des_bad_version. Flags u16 at [+6..+8] ignored at raw M5 (reserved-bits-ignored discipline). (4) Read seg_word_count u32 at [+8] into r8: xor r8, r8; mov_d r8, [rdi + 8]; cmp r8, 1; jb ws_des_bad_seg_header; cmp r8, 129; ja ws_des_bad_seg_header. Both 1 and 129 fit imm32 bit-31-clear (and 1 fits imm8; 129 is encoded as imm32 since it falls outside the imm8 sign-extend window). (5) Read record_len u32 at [+16] into r9: xor r9, r9; mov_d r9, [rdi + 16]; cmp r9, 1024; ja ws_des_record_too_large. (6) Compute data_words_expected = (record_len + 7) >> 3 into r10: mov r10, r9; add r10, 7; shr r10, 3. Validate seg_word_count == 1 + data_words_expected: mov rax, r10; add rax, 1; cmp r8, rax; jne ws_des_bad_seg_header. (7) Read struct_pointer u64 at [+24] into r11: mov r11, [rdi + 24]. Validate low 32 bits == 0 (kind=0 at bits 0..1 AND offset=0 at bits 2..31): xor rax, rax; mov_d rax, [rdi + 24]; cmp rax, 0; jne ws_des_bad_struct_ptr. Validate bits 48..63 (ptr_words) == 0: mov rax, r11; shr rax, 48; cmp rax, 0; jne ws_des_bad_struct_ptr. Validate bits 32..47 (data_words) == data_words_expected: mov rax, r11; shr rax, 32; and rax, 0xFFFF; cmp rax, r10; jne ws_des_bad_struct_ptr. The imm32 mask 0xFFFF has bit 31 clear so and-imm32 sign-extends safely to the full qword 0x000000000000FFFF. (8) Compute padded_len = data_words_expected * 8 into r11: mov r11, r10; shl r11, 3. Validate wire_len >= 32 + padded_len: mov rax, r11; add rax, 32; cmp rsi, rax; jb ws_des_bad_frame. (9) Validate out_cap >= record_len: cmp rcx, r9; jb ws_des_oos. (10) Payload copy loop: xor r11, r11; each iter cmp r11, r9; jae ws_des_done; compute src addr = wire + 32 + r11 into r8 (mov r8, rdi; add r8, 32; add r8, r11); load byte (xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [r8 + 0]); compute dst addr = out + r11 into r10 (mov r10, rdx; add r10, r11); store byte (mov_b [r10 + 0], rax); add r11, 1; jmp. Reuses r10 (data_words_expected no longer needed after geometry validation) and r11 (struct_pointer no longer needed after sub-field validation). (11) Return packed_u64 = (record_len << 32) | WS_OK: mov rax, r9; shl rax, 32; ret. r9 holds record_len throughout. Error returns: ws_des_bad_frame stamps 0x5203; ws_des_bad_magic 0x5204; ws_des_bad_version 0x5205; ws_des_bad_seg_header 0x5206; ws_des_bad_struct_ptr 0x5207; ws_des_record_too_large 0x5201; ws_des_oos 0x5202. All cmp immediates (0, 1, 7, 32, 129, 1024, magic 0x4B4E5357, mask 0xFFFF, status codes 0x5201..0x5207, field offsets 0, 4, 5, 8, 16, 24, 32) fit imm32 with bit 31 clear. No test; no and-imm64; no 2-op imul-r-imm; no multiline let. Labels prefixed ws_des_.",
      block: {
        // ---- Input validation --------------------------------------------
        // wire != 0.
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  ws_des_bad_frame;

        // wire_len >= 32.
        cmp rsi, 32;
        jb  ws_des_bad_frame;

        // out != 0.
        cmp rdx, 0;
        je  ws_des_bad_frame;

        // ---- Magic + version ---------------------------------------------
        // magic u32 at [+0] == 'WSNK' (0x4B4E5357).
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_d rax, [rdi + 0];
        cmp rax, 0x4B4E5357;                   // WS_FRAME_MAGIC
        jne ws_des_bad_magic;

        // version lo byte at [+4] == 1.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdi + 4];
        cmp rax, 1;                            // WS_FRAME_VERSION
        jne ws_des_bad_version;

        // version hi byte at [+5] == 0.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdi + 5];
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ws_des_bad_version;

        // flags u16 at [+6..+8] ignored at raw M5 (reserved-bits-ignored).

        // ---- Segment header ---------------------------------------------
        // seg_word_count u32 at [+8].
        xor r8, r8;
        mov_d r8, [rdi + 8];                   // r8 = seg_word_count

        // Validate 1 <= seg_word_count <= 129.
        cmp r8, 1;
        jb  ws_des_bad_seg_header;
        cmp r8, 129;
        ja  ws_des_bad_seg_header;

        // ---- record_len + geometry --------------------------------------
        // record_len u32 at [+16].
        xor r9, r9;
        mov_d r9, [rdi + 16];                  // r9 = record_len

        // Validate record_len <= WS_RECORD_MAX.
        cmp r9, 1024;
        ja  ws_des_record_too_large;

        // data_words_expected = (record_len + 7) >> 3.
        mov r10, r9;
        add r10, 7;
        shr r10, 3;                            // r10 = data_words_expected

        // Validate seg_word_count == 1 + data_words_expected.
        mov rax, r10;
        add rax, 1;
        cmp r8, rax;
        jne ws_des_bad_seg_header;

        // ---- Struct pointer validation ----------------------------------
        // Load whole 64-bit struct_pointer into r11 for sub-field extracts.
        mov r11, [rdi + 24];

        // Validate low 32 bits == 0 (kind=0, offset=0).
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_d rax, [rdi + 24];
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ws_des_bad_struct_ptr;

        // Validate bits 48..63 (ptr_words) == 0.
        mov rax, r11;
        shr rax, 48;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ws_des_bad_struct_ptr;

        // Validate bits 32..47 (data_words) == data_words_expected.
        mov rax, r11;
        shr rax, 32;
        and rax, 0xFFFF;                       // mask low 16 bits (imm32 safe)
        cmp rax, r10;
        jne ws_des_bad_struct_ptr;

        // ---- Wire length geometry ---------------------------------------
        // padded_len = data_words_expected * 8.
        mov r11, r10;
        shl r11, 3;                            // r11 = padded_len

        // Validate wire_len >= 32 + padded_len.
        mov rax, r11;
        add rax, 32;
        cmp rsi, rax;
        jb  ws_des_bad_frame;

        // ---- Output capacity --------------------------------------------
        // out_cap >= record_len.
        cmp rcx, r9;
        jb  ws_des_oos;

        // ---- Payload copy loop ------------------------------------------
        xor r11, r11;                          // i = 0
      ws_des_pay:
        cmp r11, r9;
        jae ws_des_done;
        // src = wire + 32 + i
        mov r8, rdi;
        add r8, 32;
        add r8, r11;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r8 + 0];
        // dst = out + i
        mov r10, rdx;
        add r10, r11;
        mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;
        add r11, 1;
        jmp ws_des_pay;

      ws_des_done:
        // Return (record_len << 32) | WS_OK (= 0).
        mov rax, r9;
        shl rax, 32;
        ret;

      ws_des_bad_frame:
        mov rax, 0x5203;                       // WS_ERR_BAD_FRAME
        ret;

      ws_des_bad_magic:
        mov rax, 0x5204;                       // WS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC
        ret;

      ws_des_bad_version:
        mov rax, 0x5205;                       // WS_ERR_BAD_VERSION
        ret;

      ws_des_bad_seg_header:
        mov rax, 0x5206;                       // WS_ERR_BAD_SEG_HEADER
        ret;

      ws_des_bad_struct_ptr:
        mov rax, 0x5207;                       // WS_ERR_BAD_STRUCT_PTR
        ret;

      ws_des_record_too_large:
        mov rax, 0x5201;                       // WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE
        ret;

      ws_des_oos:
        mov rax, 0x5202;                       // WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _ws_schema_hash(name_ptr, name_len) -> u64
  //
  //   rdi = name_ptr
  //   rsi = name_len
  //   rax = FNV-1a 64 hash of [name_ptr, name_ptr + name_len)
  //
  // FNV-1a 64 byte-for-byte identical to R222.M2's `_cmd_sig_fnv1a64`,
  // R222.M3's `_fi_fnv1a64`, R222.M5's `_cmdreg_fnv1a64`, and R229.M5's
  // inlined FNV in `_h_append`. Offset basis 0xCBF29CE484222325; prime
  // 0x100000001B3. A zero-length input returns the offset basis unchanged
  // (standard FNV behaviour).
  //
  // Pure leaf -- no callee-save push, no stack frame, no nested CALL.
  // Register plan: rax = hash accumulator; rcx = fnv_prime (held across
  // the loop); rdx = mul high half (clobbered each iteration); r8 = ptr
  // saved; r9 = len saved; r10 = loop idx; r11 = byte load scratch.
  // Labels prefixed ws_hash_.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _ws_schema_hash : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (name_ptr: u64) (name_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R223.M5 (#2670): FNV-1a 64 hash over [name_ptr, name_ptr + name_len), byte-for-byte identical to _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 (R222.M2), _fi_fnv1a64 (R222.M3), _cmdreg_fnv1a64 (R222.M5), and the inlined FNV in R229.M5's _h_append. Pure leaf; zero pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL. Load offset basis 0xCBF29CE484222325 (imm64 via REX.W+MOV; precedent _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 line 437) into rax; load fnv_prime 0x100000001B3 (imm64) into rcx, held across the loop. For each byte: xor-load byte into r11 (zero-extended via `xor r11, r11; mov_b r11, [r8 + r10]`); xor r11 into rax; xor rdx, rdx to clear the mul high half; `mul rcx` yields rdx:rax = rax * fnv_prime, we take the low 64 in rax. Advance r10 by 1; loop. Terminates on r10 >= r9. A zero-length input returns the offset basis unchanged (standard FNV behaviour). The two-register addressing form `mov_b r11, [r8 + r10]` is the FNV-family precedent and encodes directly in the pdx dialect (see _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 line 446). All cmp immediates are 0 (the i < len test uses `cmp r10, r9` + `jae`), which is imm8-safe. No test (zero-check uses `cmp`). No and-imm64. No 2-op imul-r-imm (the `mul rcx` here is the one-op unsigned-mul form; the pitfall is specifically the 2-op `imul rN, imm` form, distinct from `mul r/m64`). No multiline let. Labels prefixed ws_hash_.",
      block: {
        mov r8, rdi;                           // r8 = ptr (saved)
        mov r9, rsi;                           // r9 = len (saved)
        mov rax, 0xCBF29CE484222325;           // h = fnv_offset_basis
        mov rcx, 0x100000001B3;                // fnv_prime (held in rcx)
        xor r10, r10;                          // i = 0

      ws_hash_loop:
        cmp r10, r9;
        jae ws_hash_done;

        xor r11, r11;
        mov_b r11, [r8 + r10];                 // byte load
        xor rax, r11;                          // h ^= byte

        xor rdx, rdx;                          // clear mul high half
        mul rcx;                               // rdx:rax = rax * fnv_prime

        add r10, 1;
        jmp ws_hash_loop;

      ws_hash_done:
        ret;
      }
    }

}
